## Severity-Ranked Findings

### BLOCKER: mandatory P5 authorities are absent

The authenticated final manifest declares both readiness and compatibility
authorities above, but both exact files are absent. The successor blueprint was
not in the coordinator-authorized path set, so this review cannot reconstruct
the normative authority from a substitute. An authoritative engineering PASS
cannot be issued while the required inputs are missing.

### HIGH: fresh runtime evidence is blocked by a hard-coded `C:\Temp` requirement

The five exact replay commands all returned exit code 1 within 76 ms or less;
none reached the transaction harness. The stderr contract rejects every output
directory outside `C:\Temp`. Creating a `C:\Temp` workaround would violate the
strict boundary, so the required fresh driver-backed matrix is incomplete.
This is recorded as a bounded environment/invocation limitation, not as a
claim that the application transaction logic itself failed.

### HIGH: caller-supplied renderer handle generations are not checked against live records

The mapping validator checks each renderer handle's renderer/device generation
against the mapping fence (`render/editor/SceneRuntimePublisher.cpp:648-675`),
but does not compare the handle index/generation to the live concrete record.
Stable-ID transition validation only compares whether entries exist before and
after (`render/editor/SceneRuntimePublisher.cpp:413-425`).

The live scene-object record does contain a typed generation-bearing handle
(`render/GPUDrivenRenderer.cpp:1200-1207`), yet `validateLiveHandles()` checks
only source-ID existence for scene objects (`render/GPUDrivenRenderer.cpp:1596-1607`).
Camera and light checks verify kind and projected asset-index range only
(`render/GPUDrivenRenderer.cpp:1633-1644`). The private generation used to
construct candidate handles is derived from the caller mapping's maximum
generation (`render/GPUDrivenRenderer.cpp:1375-1388`), so an update can carry a
different object generation while retaining a valid fence generation. The
candidate then copies the target mapping and publishes it
(`render/GPUDrivenRenderer.cpp:1758-1786`), allowing a forged/stale generation
to become the renderer-owned identity without a live-record equality check.

The public concrete snapshot exposes mesh, texture, material, and scene-object
handle vectors but no camera/light handle vectors
(`render/GPUDrivenRenderer.h:303-335`). Positive camera/light effect tests do
not provide a negative stale-generation case (`tests/editor/editor_renderer_integration_tests.cpp:3030-3162`).
This leaves the Section 7.1 requirement for actual generation-safe renderer
identity unproven and can bind a reused index to the wrong logical object.

